      "problem": "Consider the equation $-2{x}^{2}+6x-5=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?",
      "worked_solution": "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ and $c=-5,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={6}^{2}-4(-2)(-5) \\\\  & =36-40 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions.",
